"The postoperative prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include a negative surgical margin (0.0), no seminal vesicle invasion (0.0), absence of lymphovascular invasion (0.0), and a low preoperative PSA of 2.5. The surgical pathology showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pT2 stage with no extraprostatic extension. Countervailing factors include the absence of lymph node removal, as no nodes were removed, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status, as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The biopsy finding, showing cribriform pattern, intraductal carcinoma, and perineural invasion, contrasts with the negative surgical margins. The primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as this information is missing. Postoperative PSA levels and follow-up are critical for assessing recurrence risk. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of local control. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory would significantly revise the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 58.11 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.77–78.45 months; its coverage has not been validated."
